    "materials": {
      "Output & Printing": {
        "text": "**Output** is how a program shows results. Learn your language's print/output statement and practice printing text (strings, in quotes) and numbers. This is the foundation you'll use to inspect everything else you build.",
        "code": "@main def run() = println(\"Hello World\")"
      },
      "Variables": {
        "text": "Learn the core ideas of this module, then practice with the challenges below.",
        "code": "@main def run() = {\n  val x = 5\n  println(x)\n}"
      },
      "Operators & Math": {
        "text": "**Operators** combine values. Arithmetic: `+` `-` `*` `/` and `%` (remainder). Expressions follow normal math precedence — multiplication and division before addition and subtraction. Integer vs. floating-point division can behave differently, so watch your types.\n\n**Key idea:** an expression is evaluated to a single value before it's used or printed.",
        "code": "@main def run() = println(7 + 6)"
      },
      "Conditionals": {
        "text": "**Conditionals** let a program make decisions. A *comparison* (`>`, `<`, `==`, `!=`) produces a boolean, and `if` / `else` runs different code depending on the result. The `%` (modulo) operator is a workhorse here — `n % 2` tells you if a number is even or odd.\n\n**Key idea:** branch your logic based on true/false tests.",
        "code": "@main def run() = {\n  val n = 7\n  if (n > 0) println(\"positive\") else println(\"not positive\")\n}"
      },
      "Loops": {
        "text": "**Loops** repeat work. A `for` loop runs a known number of times by walking a counter through a range; a `while` loop runs as long as a condition stays true. Loops often build a *running total* or transform each item. Always make sure the loop can end — or you'll spin forever.\n\n**Key idea:** repeat a block instead of copy-pasting it.",
        "code": "@main def run() = for (i <- 1 to 3) println(i)"
      },
      "Functions": {
        "text": "**Functions** package reusable logic. They take **parameters** (inputs), do work, and usually **return** a result. Define a function once and call it as many times as you like with different arguments. Functions make code shorter, clearer, and testable.\n\n**Key idea:** name a piece of logic so you can reuse it.",
        "code": "def add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b\n@main def run() = println(add(20, 22))"
      },
      "Comments & Syntax": {
        "text": "**Comments** are notes the compiler ignores — they explain *why* code does something. Every language has its own comment marker (`//`, `#`, `--`, `;`). Good comments make code readable for your future self and teammates.",
        "code": "// this is a comment\n@main def run() = ()"
      }
    },
    "guide": [
      {
        "title": "1. Introduction",
        "body": "Welcome to the complete **Scala** guide. Functional meets OOP on the JVM.\n\n## How to use this guide\nRead each topic, study the examples, then practice with the challenges in that module. Come back here whenever you need a refresher.",
        "examples": []
      },
      {
        "title": "2. Output & Printing",
        "body": "**Output** is how a program shows results. Learn your language's print/output statement and practice printing text (strings, in quotes) and numbers. This is the foundation you'll use to inspect everything else you build.",
        "examples": [
          "@main def run() = println(\"Hello World\")",
          "@main def run() = println(\"Welcome\")",
          "@main def run() = println(\"I love coding\")",
          "@main def run() = println(\"Keep going\")"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "3. Variables",
        "body": "Learn the core ideas of this module, then practice with the challenges below.",
        "examples": [
          "@main def run() = {\n  val x = 5\n  println(x)\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val count = 10\n  println(count)\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val age = 30\n  println(age)\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val score = 99\n  println(score)\n}"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "4. Operators & Math",
        "body": "**Operators** combine values. Arithmetic: `+` `-` `*` `/` and `%` (remainder). Expressions follow normal math precedence — multiplication and division before addition and subtraction. Integer vs. floating-point division can behave differently, so watch your types.\n\n**Key idea:** an expression is evaluated to a single value before it's used or printed.",
        "examples": [
          "@main def run() = println(7 + 6)",
          "@main def run() = println(13 + 8)",
          "@main def run() = println(20 - 5)",
          "@main def run() = println(9 * 9)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "5. Conditionals",
        "body": "**Conditionals** let a program make decisions. A *comparison* (`>`, `<`, `==`, `!=`) produces a boolean, and `if` / `else` runs different code depending on the result. The `%` (modulo) operator is a workhorse here — `n % 2` tells you if a number is even or odd.\n\n**Key idea:** branch your logic based on true/false tests.",
        "examples": [
          "@main def run() = {\n  val n = 7\n  if (n > 0) println(\"positive\") else println(\"not positive\")\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val n = 10\n  if (n > 0) println(\"positive\") else println(\"not positive\")\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val n = 0\n  if (n > 0) println(\"positive\") else println(\"not positive\")\n}",
          "@main def run() = {\n  val n = 42\n  if (n > 0) println(\"positive\") else println(\"not positive\")\n}"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "6. Loops",
        "body": "**Loops** repeat work. A `for` loop runs a known number of times by walking a counter through a range; a `while` loop runs as long as a condition stays true. Loops often build a *running total* or transform each item. Always make sure the loop can end — or you'll spin forever.\n\n**Key idea:** repeat a block instead of copy-pasting it.",
        "examples": [
          "@main def run() = for (i <- 1 to 3) println(i)",
          "@main def run() = for (i <- 1 to 4) println(i)",
          "@main def run() = for (i <- 1 to 5) println(i)",
          "@main def run() = for (i <- 1 to 6) println(i)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "7. Functions",
        "body": "**Functions** package reusable logic. They take **parameters** (inputs), do work, and usually **return** a result. Define a function once and call it as many times as you like with different arguments. Functions make code shorter, clearer, and testable.\n\n**Key idea:** name a piece of logic so you can reuse it.",
        "examples": [
          "def add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b\n@main def run() = println(add(20, 22))",
          "def add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b\n@main def run() = println(add(1, 1))",
          "def add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b\n@main def run() = println(add(50, 50))",
          "def add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b\n@main def run() = println(add(7, 8))"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "8. Comments & Syntax",
        "body": "**Comments** are notes the compiler ignores — they explain *why* code does something. Every language has its own comment marker (`//`, `#`, `--`, `;`). Good comments make code readable for your future self and teammates.",
        "examples": [
          "// this is a comment\n@main def run() = ()",
          "// this is a comment\n@main def run() = ()",
          "// this is a comment\n@main def run() = ()",
          "// this is a comment\n@main def run() = ()"
        ]
      }
    ]
